artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider mathematical models of adaptive cancer therapy in which a tumor is composed of two (or more) cell populations: treatment-sensitive cells S(t) and treatment-resistant cells R(t), interacting through ecological competition. Classical formulations include frequency-dependent and density-dependent Lotka–Volterra-type systems such as
  dS/dt = r_S S (1 − (S + α_{SR} R)/K) − μ(t) S,
  dR/dt = r_R R (1 − (R + α_{RS} S)/K),
where r_S, r_R are intrinsic growth rates, α_{SR}, α_{RS} are interspecific competition coefficients, K is a carrying capacity, and μ(t) is a (possibly adaptive) treatment schedule. A core hypothesis underlying adaptive therapy is that R-cells are less competitive than S-cells in the absence of treatment (a fitness cost of resistance).

Provide a quantitative characterization, for biologically realistic tumors, of:
(a) the functional form of competition between S and R cells (e.g., frequency-dependent vs. density-dependent vs. resource-explicit), including identification of which mechanistic terms (shared nutrients, oxygen, space) drive the competition;
(b) whether R-cells indeed carry a measurable fitness cost relative to S-cells when treatment is absent, and the magnitude of α_{RS}, α_{SR}, r_R/r_S that this implies;
(c) how spatial structure, finite interaction ranges, tumor architecture, and the microenvironment modify these competition parameters, e.g., by replacing well-mixed ODEs with spatially explicit PDE, agent-based, or network models;
(d) whether non-competitive ecological interactions (mutualism, commensalism, parasitism, public goods) between S and R play a sufficiently important role that purely competitive frequency- or density-dependent two-population models fail to predict therapeutic outcomes, and if so, what minimal model extensions are required.

